salamat | definition | Salamat in Filipino is the standard thank you, and the fuller form acknowledges the specific thing rather than offering a vague thanks, so salamat sa pagtulong mo is thank you for your help. The reply is not a reciprocal thanks, because that implies a further favour being given, and the usual answers are walay ikaw, which literally says I have nothing and means there is no need, or mao gyud kaayo, which does mean you are welcome and is used after a meal. In Filipino the pronoun changes who you are talking to: ka is polite and used to an older person or someone being respected, while kayo is the plural you. A learner who uses kayo to a single elder will sound either too familiar or simply wrong depending on the relationship, so the distinction is worth learning early rather than treating it as optional politeness. | thank you, you are welcome, walay ikaw, mao gyud kaayo, polite, reply | anong meaning ng salamat, paano magsalabat ng salamat, how do you reply to thank you | salamat | salamat
